    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In order to understand the mechanical origin of natural fractures associated with folding, and to predict fracture orientation and spatial density in reservoirs, we present a new method based on composite plate theory. In this method the finite difference method is used to calculate the principal curvatures and the principal strains based on structural elevations (structure contours) obtained from seismic data of sedimentary rock. Because joint spacing is closely related to the strain in layers, the relationship between joint spacing and the maximum tensile strain is used to predict the fracture spacing distribution and orientation in reservoirs. Gaussian curvature, the product of the principal curvatures has been used to predict regions of high fracture density due to folding. A comparison between Gaussian curvature and the method described here is made here, and shows that the composite plate method compensates for some deficiencies of Gaussian curvature, for example, Gaussian curvature is not a good measure of the magnitude of the strain for cylindrical folds. An example, Goose Egg dome located at the west end of Casper mountain in central Wyoming, is used to illustrate how the method can be applied to predict regions of high fracture density in the subsurface after calibration using outcrop data.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
